acidity of hydrogen ions in an aqueous

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:18.849
solution, meaning that whenever we have
any type of solution that you might

00:00:18.849 --> 00:00:23.759
have around the household or in the
chemistry lab, they have a pH to them.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.819
Meaning they have so much acidity to them.

00:00:26.255 --> 00:00:29.365
So this is all about the
potential of hydrogen.

00:00:29.715 --> 00:00:32.935
So how much hydrogen ions do
we have within a solution?

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.540
So if I, for pH, this is a scale
that is a very simple range.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.720
It goes from zero to 14.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.430
It is a simple scale.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:44.780
So that way we can easily be able to
relate it to normal numbers that you

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.250
can count on your hand, hands and toes.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:51.180
So if we have a scale like this,
the way that it generally works

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.000
is it goes from zero to 14.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:58.179
Meaning that we have a range
of how acidic something can be.

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:02.889
So a pH of 7 is usually where that
is, where we all want to start.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.309
That is where we have water.

00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:07.850
That is just a normal pH of 7.

00:01:08.209 --> 00:01:09.270
Meaning this is neutral.

00:01:09.279 --> 00:01:11.484
So you can drink, drinking
water and it is fine.

00:01:11.835 --> 00:01:13.045
generally safe for you to do.

00:01:13.505 --> 00:01:19.835
Now, as you go into the more acidic side,
that is when the pH is going less than 7.

00:01:19.845 --> 00:01:24.294
So the lower that number, the
more acidic something is, meaning

00:01:24.335 --> 00:01:29.425
the higher that that hydrogen ion
concentration is, within a solution.

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.345
So if you kind of change some of
those, some of those things that you

